<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>He is thirsty, up at night to pass urine, tired and losing weight, and wants to know whether he truly has diabetes and what kind. [S]</item><item>He wants any treatment to fit his monastic life: his diet, his fasts and his travel. [S]</item><item>He has never been able to fall asleep easily, and broken nights now leave him exhausted. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>He was born Narendranath (Narendra Nath) Datta in an affluent family in Calcutta (Kolkata) on 12 January 1863. His father, Vishwanath Datta, was a successful attorney; his mother was Bhuvaneshwari Devi. [D]</item><item>He met Sri Ramakrishna at Dakshineswar in November 1881 and became his disciple; in 1887 he and his brother disciples took the formal vows of sannyasa and new monastic names. [D]</item><item>He left Bombay for America on 31 May 1893 and spoke at the World&apos;s Parliament of Religions in Chicago in September 1893, which made him famous; he then spent nearly three and a half years teaching Vedanta, mostly in the eastern United States and London, and returned to India in January 1897. [D]</item><item>On 1 May 1897, at a meeting of the monastic and lay devotees of Sri Ramakrishna in Calcutta, he founded the Ramakrishna Mission Association; in February 1898 the monastery moved to a garden house at Belur on the west bank of the Ganga, and land for the Belur Math was bought in early 1898. [D]</item><item>In March and April 1897, soon after his return to India, he wrote that he had diabetes. On 16 March he wrote: &quot;The work has broken me down completely, and I have got &apos;diabetes&apos;, an incurable disease&quot;; on 26 March: &quot;The result of this steady work in the West and the tremendous work of a month in India upon the Bengalee constitution is &apos;diabetes&apos;. It is a hereditary foe,&quot; adding that &quot;Eating only meat and drinking no water seems to be the only way to prolong life&quot;; and on 7 April that he had been &quot;attacked by &apos;Diabetes&apos;, a fell disease&quot; and had gone to Darjeeling, cool and &quot;very good for the disease,&quot; where the doctors did not want him to move about. A biography says he went to Darjeeling on the advice of physicians. [D]</item><item>On 29 May 1897, from Almora, he wrote to a physician disciple that he was taking the medicines sent to him, riding morning and evening and feeling very strong, but that &quot;I never in my life could sleep as soon as I got into bed. I must toss for at least two hours,&quot; that he felt hot after the evening meal, that &quot;There has not been any thirst even though the days are fearfully hot,&quot; and that he was afraid of &quot;getting fat on a too much milk diet.&quot; [D]</item></list><paragraph>10 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.20" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11348-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Past illness note"/>
    <title>Past Medical History</title>
    <text><paragraph>Historical biography with true dates. These events are not part of the synthetic care timeline.</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Historical date</th><th>History</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>March 1897</td><td>Diabetes, as he described it in his letters, after his return to India at about 34; physicians advised the cool air of Darjeeling and rest; he wrote that eating only meat and drinking no water seemed the only way to prolong his life; how it was diagnosed is not recorded</td><td>D (CLM-0005)</td></tr><tr><td>May 29, 1897</td><td>Lifelong difficulty falling asleep, in his own words: unable to sleep on getting into bed, tossing for at least two hours</td><td>D (CLM-0006)</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

